dsp: a masked interrupt must not stop the peripherals

Once injectInterrupt has swapped m_interruptFunc, returning early for a masked
vector means the peripherals are never serviced again for as long as the mask
holds — and the ESAI/ESSI clocks live inside them. On silicon a masked interrupt
only defers the service routine: the flag stays pending and every peripheral
keeps clocking regardless of the core's IPL.

diff --git a/source/dsp56kEmu/dsp.cpp b/source/dsp56kEmu/dsp.cpp
index db792a8..3867298 100644
--- a/source/dsp56kEmu/dsp.cpp
+++ b/source/dsp56kEmu/dsp.cpp
@@ -178,7 +178,22 @@ namespace dsp56k
 		const auto vba = interrupt;
 
 		if(isInterruptMasked(vba))
+		{
+			// A masked vector must not stop the chip.
+			//
+			// m_interruptFunc has already been swapped to this function by
+			// injectInterrupt, so returning here without servicing the peripherals
+			// means they are never serviced again for as long as the mask holds --
+			// and the ESAI/ESSI clocks live inside them. On real silicon a masked
+			// interrupt only defers the SERVICE ROUTINE: the flag stays pending and
+			// every peripheral keeps clocking regardless of the core's IPL.
+			//
+			// Observed on the Elektron Octatrack's DSP payload: the ESAI transmitted
+			// exactly one frame in 202 million retired instructions, while the DSP
+			// spun on a frame sync that could no longer arrive.
+			m_execPeripheralsFunc(this);
 			return;
+		}
 
 		// it is important that the processing mode is switched first before popping the vector to prevent a possible race condition in hasPendingInterrupt()
 		{
